WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.440
Antonio Criminisi: Look, our work is very,
very practical.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.820
We want to develop technology to help oncologists,
radiologists and, eventually surgeons, as well.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.940
That’s all. So, it is a productivity tool.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.420
They are not the type of AI that people say
will take over the world at all.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.920
They are just very practical, concrete tools
to help reduce cost and save time.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:22.920
(Music plays)

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.119
Host: You’re listening to the Microsoft
Research podcast.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.340
A show that brings you closer to the cutting-edge
of technology research and the scientists behind it.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.880
I’m your host, Gretchen Huizinga.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.300
(Music plays)

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.180
Host: With all the sensational headlines about
artificial intelligence, it’s reassuring

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.790
to know that some of the world’s most brilliant
minds are developing AI systems for entirely

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.230
practical reasons.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:53.930
One of those minds belongs to Dr. Antonio
Criminisi, a Principal Researcher at Microsoft

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.180
Research in Cambridge, England.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.740
And one of those reasons is to
help medical professionals provide better

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.520
healthcare for their patients.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:07.640
Today, Dr. Criminisi talks about Project InnerEye,
an innovative machine learning tool that helps

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.630
radiologists identify and analyze 3-D images
of cancerous tumors.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.130
He also gives us some insight into his work
on deep neural decision forests and tells

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.590
us how gaming algorithms made their way into
medical technology, moving from gamer to patient,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.789
and turning outside-in imaging… inside-out.

00:01:26.789 --> 00:01:31.509
That and much more, on this episode of the
Microsoft Research Podcast.

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:36.240
(music)

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.479
Host: Thanks Antonio, again for joining us
from MSR Cambridge in the UK via Skype.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:42.689
It’s great to have you with us.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:44.600
Antonio Criminisi: No problem.
Thank you.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:49.139
Host: So, you’re a principal researcher
on InnerEye AI for cancer which uses machine

00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:51.600
learning algorithms to treat cancer.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.530
So, give us an overview of the work you are
doing in computer vision and medical imaging

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:57.530
analysis.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.529
Antonio Criminisi: Sure, my pleasure.

00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:06.369
So, what we do in project InnerEye is, we
apply state-of-the-art machine learning technology

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:09.209
for the analysis of radiological images.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:16.590
In particular here, we’re talking about
CT as in Computer Tomography, and MR as in

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.500
Magnetic Resonance images.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.580
And we’re looking, specifically, at images
of patients who have already been diagnosed

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.240
with some form of cancer, unfortunately.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:35.769
And what the technology does is, analyzes
those images, at a pixel-by-pixel level, to

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:38.920
figure out exactly where the tumor is.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:46.020
But also, to do what’s called the delineation
or contouring, of organs around the tumor.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.980
They are called organs-at-risk.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:53.680
And the reason why this is important is because,
for instance, in radiation therapy, you need

00:02:53.689 --> 00:03:01.489
to instruct the machine that delivers the
radiation, the therapy, to exactly where the

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:07.890
target is, i.e., the tumor, but also the organs
that need to be spared from nasty radiation.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:15.459
This is normally a process that is done manually,
with somewhat archaic tools.

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:19.909
And we can help, precisely in that area, to
make the delineation, the contouring, and

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:24.110
therefore, the radiotherapy planning, a lot
quicker and also more cost-effective.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:32.349
Host: So tell us how gaming technology algorithms
are now working in medical technology.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.810
How did you go from gamer to patient and inside-out
imaging to outside-in imaging?

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.060
Antonio Criminisi: Sure.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.300
So, our expertise is in machine learning.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:47.670
So, for a decade or more, we’ve been working
on experimenting with new, better, more efficient,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:52.810
more accurate machine learning algorithms
for doing predictions from images.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:55.749
And those are pretty much any type of images.

00:03:55.749 --> 00:04:02.680
It could be your holiday snaps, it could be
videos, or it could be medical imaging.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:09.590
So, when we were working on the technology,
we developed some algorithms which turned

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.120
out to be both accurate and particularly efficient.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.060
And at that moment, we thought, “Hey, if
these algorithms work on decked images from

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:25.440
the outside of a person, in that case a player,
perhaps they can also work on images where

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.190
we are looking at the inside of a patient
body in that case.”

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.199
And that’s where the project, you know,
was born, really.

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:38.460
Host: So, what are the unique challenges that
radiologists and clinicians face that your

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.030
work helps address?

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.680
You mentioned delineation earlier.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.080
You also talked about quantification at one point.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.150
Can you talk about how your work has an impact
on these two big ideas?

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.110
Antonio Criminisi: Yes, absolutely.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:59.840
So, there are many medical experts in modern
hospitals who are faced with a number of issues,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.020
and normally they spend an enormous amount
of time trying to tackle those issues.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.259
In particular we already mentioned the work
of radiation oncologists where they need to

00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:14.830
delineate, with great accuracy, the tumor
and the organs-at-risk so that they can deliver

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.470
safe and effective, you know, therapy.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.509
In this case we’re talking about radiotherapy.

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:23.229
On the other hand, there is radiologists who
have got a very different role.

00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:30.470
In most cases, radiologists, they look at
images of patients and they need to assess

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:35.919
what they are looking at; the disease, not
only the type of disease, but also whether

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:40.890
the disease is progressing over time or it
is responding to the treatment.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:46.160
And, unfortunately, nowadays, they do not
have very good tools for doing the latter,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.940
this, you know, assessment and the
quantification of the disease.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:55.539
There are no very good quantification tools
where you can actually measure, say, the volume

00:05:55.539 --> 00:05:58.300
of the tumor from a radiological image.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.480
And that’s where we can help.
That’s the idea.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.440
That potentially our technology can be embedded
within a radiologist’s workflow and help

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.960
translate those radiological
images into measuring devices.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:15.241
That’s our goal, turning those images into
measuring devices where the radiologists can

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:20.550
actually write in the radiological report,
“This is how big the tumor is today.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.780
This is how big it was last week.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.500
This is how big it was two weeks ago.”

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:30.710
And so, they can then plot the path of progression
of the disease with great accuracy and rigor.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:36.000
Host: And that’s super important in treating
cancer, is to see how aggressive tumors are, yeah?

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.340
Antonio Criminisi: Yeah, absolutely.
That’s just one of the examples.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:43.720
Where, also, it could be used for instance
to figure out which drug works best, right?

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:50.880
So, if I’m trialing different drugs, I want
to know which one is more effective, faster-acting,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.099
and so on. Those are just some
of many possible examples.

00:06:54.099 --> 00:07:00.800
Host: You know, it’s such a fascinating
field that you’re working in and so important

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:01.800
for so many people.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.389
I mean, I don’t know anyone who hasn’t
been touched by cancer…

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:10.970
As you are looking at an image, you say it’s
important that people can tell the difference

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.880
between the bad tissue and the good tissue.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:15.970
How does it do that?

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:16.970
Antonio Criminisi: Right.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:23.669
So, as all machine-learning algorithms – the
algorithm needs to be optimized or trained.

00:07:23.669 --> 00:07:30.660
And so, what you do is, you collect a number
of anonymized images which show the same type

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.120
of cancer, solely tumor, for example.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.919
And you have experts delineating the tumor
and delineating the different organs-at-risk

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:41.270
around the tumor.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:46.800
And then you feed that to an algorithm who
looks at a variety of patterns within the image:

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.699
the intensity or the brightness of
the pixels, color, if you have color images.

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:55.000
But more importantly, the texture around them.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:02.100
And also, what we call semantic context, i.e.,
a pixel in the heart is defined as such,

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.760
not just because of the way it looks.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.640
Because that wouldn’t be sufficient.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.879
There is a lot of pixels in the human body;
images of the human body that look alike.

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:17.879
And so, it’s much more important to look
around and see whether that pixel resides

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.280
in-between two very dark regions which normally
represent the lungs. Right?

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:27.900
So, if I know that I can see the lungs, the
left lung and the right lung, then I know

00:08:27.909 --> 00:08:30.200
in-between those there should be,
you know, the heart.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.220
You know, I expect the heart to be
there of course.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.690
So, other structures like the
spine and so on.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.770
But that gives you a little hint of how these
techniques work and what they do.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:45.769
They look, not just to the pixels and features
extracted from the pixels or voxels, but also,

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:52.430
they look around to see whether there are
other patterns that reinforce - the reference

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:56.680
voxel should be the heart or the pelvis or
the prostate and so on.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:00.220
Host: So, your algorithms have to
train on good tissue as well as bad

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.930
tissue so that it knows the difference…

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:02.930
Antonio Criminisi: That’s right.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:07.220
They have to look at the whole image really
to make sure that they identified the correct

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.750
region and they classify the correct
region as such.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:15.550
Host: That’s fascinating because when you
are being treated for cancer, I would imagine,

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:17.680
you know, “Please don’t wreck
the other stuff.”

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.320
I mean, that’s what people are looking for
is the magic bullet to only kill cancer and

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.720
not destroy everything else
about your body, right?

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.450
Antonio Criminisi: That’s right, absolutely.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:30.950
Host: When we talked about what machines are
good at and what humans are good at, how does

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:38.770
this particular machine-learning technique
augment what humans are already doing in radiology

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:39.959
and cancer treatment?

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.300
Antonio Criminisi: Yes, that’s a very, very
good question.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:48.680
So, we’re very proud of the fact that we’re
designing the technology around medical experts.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.260
We are working with a number of medical experts
who are giving us a lot of instructions and guidelines.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:59.760
And so, for instance, through this process,
we have learned very early on that doctors

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:07.220
are extremely good, in most cases, at the
task of diagnosis, which means looking at,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:12.110
you know, radiological images and figuring
out what is wrong with their patient.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:16.940
So very often, in most cases, they can look
at an MR image of a patient’s brain and

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:23.480
very quickly say, “That looks like a glioblastoma,”
or another type of brain tumor for instance.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.690
And that is a very quick process.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.790
Again, there are edge cases, not everything
is so easy.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:31.010
But for the most part, that’s quick.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:37.550
But what we have discovered, through working
with many clinicians, is that measuring tools,

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:38.880
that’s the problem.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.940
That’s what they do not have.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:47.779
And so, our technology augments their skills,
or amplifies their skills by providing expert

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:52.950
doctors or radiologists, in this case, with
measuring tools, something that they desperately

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.400
need and they do not have right now.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.680
(music plays)

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.120
Host: Let’s switch over to your concept
of decision forests.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:13.680
Most of us have heard of decision trees, but
decision forests is fascinating to me, particularly

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.130
with your novel approach to machine-learning,
what you called Deep Neural Decision Forests

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.079
or DNDFs.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:24.300
Explain the difference between the forest
and the trees… and what’s unique about DNDFs.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.280
Antonio Criminisi: Sure.
I should also set

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:30.930
all of this in the background of, you know,
the most modern wave of machine-learning

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.490
which goes under the name of “deep learning.”

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:38.560
The whole world is talking about deep learning,
and in particular they are talking about convolutional

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.270
neural networks as a very effective
and accurate technology.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:48.470
We know, and we’re very well-versed, both
with convolution neural networks, but also

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:51.190
with decision trees and decision forests.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:55.920
And we have explored advantages and disadvantages
of both techniques.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:03.589
And we believe that we are onto a new set
of techniques which we call deep forests,

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:06.820
where we manage to marry the benefits of both worlds.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:12.680
And in fact, it looks like, from a theoretical
and algorithmic point-of-view, those two worlds

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:15.450
are really two ends of a continuous spectrum.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.850
They are not so different from one another.

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:23.320
But to go back to your question, a decision
forest is a collection of decision trees,

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:28.980
in practice, where those decision trees are
all slightly different from one another, and

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:36.220
the advantage of using a collection of trees
translates into better generalization,

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:42.000
which is this issue of, “Okay I’ve got a machine
learning algorithm that works very well on

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.029
the training data, but what guarantees do
I have that they would work equally well on

00:12:47.029 --> 00:12:52.180
previously unseen data, what goes under the
name of testing data?”

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:58.339
And so, the use of un-sampled techniques,
i.e., a decision forest, gives us a little

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:00.510
bit more guarantees in that sense.

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:04.190
Host: How would you frame the work
that you’re doing?

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:07.510
What specific targets are you aiming at with
what you are doing?

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:08.790
Antonio Criminisi: Yeah, good question.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.640
Look, our work is very, very practical, okay?

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:17.940
So, we want to develop technology to help
oncologists, radiologists and eventually surgeons,

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.880
as well. That’s all.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:22.140
So, it is a productivity tool.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.649
Like, Microsoft is particularly good at delivering
productivity tools.

00:13:25.649 --> 00:13:29.839
They are not the type of AI that people say
will take over the world at all.

00:13:29.839 --> 00:13:34.579
They are just very practical, concrete tools
to help reduce cost and save time.

00:13:34.579 --> 00:13:39.070
Host: Yeah, and that’s one of the – you
know, there’s a lot of scary headlines out

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:43.100
there about AI taking over the world or at
least getting us all fired.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:48.840
And so, as you frame this as a tool to help
radiologists in what they are already good at,

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:54.380
and augment them, I hear that over and
over at MSR, this “augment versus replace.”

00:13:54.389 --> 00:13:57.839
I find that fascinating with what you are
doing with InnerEye.

00:13:57.839 --> 00:13:59.339
Antonio Criminisi: Yes, thank you.

00:13:59.339 --> 00:14:03.230
Host: What other broader potential might 
this tool have?

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:08.790
Is it really focused just on cancer and radiology
and that kind of thing, or do you see applications

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.250
in other areas of medical technology as well?

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:19.670
Antonio Criminisi: We, in our team, are focusing
on image analytics, and so any clinical workflow

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:27.060
that can potentially use images of different
types would, in theory, benefit from this technology.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:34.500
And so, you could think of pathological images,
you could think of malaria, which is hematology

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:37.380
images. You could think of 2-D x-rays.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:41.070
You could think of, you know,
higher dimensional images.

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:44.040
There are many, many, many options.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:48.990
Obviously in our team, we want to be concrete
and deliver your value, so we are starting

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:55.040
small, and radiotherapy area is our target
initial domain really.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:01.020
Host: So, as you are working in this area
and that’s your goal, do you have any kind

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:04.790
of corpus of evidence or data that this kind
of machine-learning technologies are actually

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:09.209
working and helping the radiologists and cancer
treatment professionals?

00:15:09.209 --> 00:15:10.320
Antonio Criminisi: Yes.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.769
We are gathering that evidence as we speak.

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:17.640
And so through our many collaborators throughout
the world, we are working with many different

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:23.070
hospitals in many continents to make sure
the technology that we are building together

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:28.180
works, you know, across different countries,
not just, you know, in the UK, not just in

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:30.930
the US, but you know, as much as possible
for everybody.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:37.020
And, we are starting to get evidence through
our partners that the technology is starting

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:40.490
to get really good, and we keep partnering
with them to make it even better.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:44.510
Host: Talk about the accessibility of your
technology.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:48.819
How does a medical professional get access
to it, how do they use it?

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:55.500
Antonio Criminisi: So, we are working with
partners who goes often under the name of ISVs.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:16:02.040
And so, we are going to deploy our technologies
through third-party software providers.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:09.760
So, there are many corporations, many companies
who are very, very good at building what’s

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.390
called medical devices, software-only medical
devices, in many cases.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:21.250
And so, they are the people, they are the
companies who then sell on those devices to

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.389
healthcare providers.

00:16:22.389 --> 00:16:30.980
What we do, is work with those software providers
and we provide them with our own state-of-the-art

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:36.089
AI machine-learning technology to make those
products better for their end customers.

00:16:36.089 --> 00:16:40.260
Host: So, they incorporate what you’ve done
into their products and then pass that on to them.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:41.100
Antonio Criminisi: Absolutely.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.140
Host: Okay, that’s interesting.

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:42.840
Antonio Criminisi: Absolutely.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:49.120
So, the technology we are developing will
be exposed as a set of Azure services.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:53.339
From a medical point-of-view, you can think
of them as medical components which then get

00:16:53.339 --> 00:16:57.220
incorporated into a third party,
end-to-end product.

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:01.040
And that way, we can make our partners better.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:07.910
Host: So, as we talk about work in the medical
field, there’s been discussion about the

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:11.440
delicate balance between progress and privacy.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:14.790
And it’s particularly acute when you are
dealing with sensitive health data.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:20.420
Are there any challenges you face with this
technology in light of legislative or policy safeguards?

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:21.500
Antonio Criminisi: Absolutely.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:25.480
So, you know, we are dealing with very sensitive
patient information here.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.230
We are talking about radiological images.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:32.010
And so, we need to be extremely careful in
the way we handle them.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:37.360
And at Microsoft, we are incredibly, you know,
aware of all the issues to do with patient

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.320
privacy, anonymity and so on.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:45.170
And so, we make sure that we comply with all
regulations, but we go beyond there.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:49.640
We are super-transparent with what we do with
those patient images.

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:57.340
As an example, our algorithms have been designed
to need only the pixels, nothing else.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:18:03.860
In order to train algorithms and to optimize
them, for them to deliver value, all we need

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:05.340
is the pixel information.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:11.760
We don’t need any patient-related information,
or any information related to the hospital of origin.

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:13.360
And that’s a big advantage for us.

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:19.400
You know, the algorithms have been designed
precisely to be as strict and rigorous as

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.120
possible in terms of preserving
the patient’s privacy.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:28.620
Host: Are you running into any of the same
issues with InnerEye, with the GDPR regulations?

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:33.810
Antonio Criminisi: We are very well aware
of the GDPR and for InnerEye, we are already

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:35.520
compliant with GDPR.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:39.990
We know exactly what the regulation requires
and does not require.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:45.660
As a quick example, again, all the data that
is ingested by our training algorithms is

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.590
completely anonymous.

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:54.560
It is impossible to go from the pixels that
we have to the identity of the patient even

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:59.240
if there was an attack and someone maliciously
wanted to record the ID of the patient,

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:01.300
it wouldn’t be possible to do so.

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:02.420
Host: That’s awesome.

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:06.100
You refer to pixels and I saw a term
called voxels.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.100
Antonio Criminisi: That’s right.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:08.400
Host: Tell the difference between those.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:12.220
Antonio Criminisi: A voxel is a 3-D pixel.
That’s all.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:16.420
Radiological images often come
in a 3-dimensional format.

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:22.440
Think of a 3-dimensional grid in space, and
so each element in that grid rather than being

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:26.180
called a pixel is called a voxel.
That’s the only difference.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:34.520
(music plays)

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:43.500
Host: So, if I’m a person that’s interested
in medical science, I might not consider computer

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:50.700
science as a way to get to my career goal,
but this feels like it’s kind of a crossover

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:53.100
between the two.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:58.620
Can you explain how maybe somebody interested
in working on diseases and helping in that

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:02.080
area would find computer science as a good
path to that?

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:06.590
Antonio Criminisi: Yeah, so as you know computer
science is everywhere nowadays, right?

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:10.450
There aren’t many fields which haven’t
been touched by computer science.

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:15.340
And the same applies to medicine, and in particular
to radiology.

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:21.120
And I see more and more radiologists being
extremely savvy about computer technology,

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:28.480
being able to write code and program themselves
into a little bit or maybe a lot of image

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:30.440
analysis, themselves.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:38.000
So, this is really refreshing to see because
obviously the more crosstalk there is between

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:43.350
pure computer scientists and say pure oncologists,
the better for both worlds.

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:48.580
Host: So, what was your path to medical image
analysis research, Antonio?

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:53.100
Antonio Criminisi: I’m an engineer and I
have always been passionate about images.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:59.950
Therefore, I ended up doing a PhD in computer
vision, which is everything to do with algorithms

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:03.640
for analysis of any type of images.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:09.860
And then I became passionate about applying
those techniques to radiological images because

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:13.760
I clearly saw an immediate benefit there for
patients.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:20.730
Then, you know, within Microsoft, I was fortunate
enough to be allowed to start looking to that

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:27.310
space a little bit more deeply and work with
radiologists and hospitals across the globe.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:31.930
And I found it extremely fascinating and refreshing
and inspirational as well.

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:35.440
Host: How did you end up at Microsoft Research
in the UK?

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:41.600
Antonio Criminisi: I did a PhD in Oxford in
the UK; then I was hired into Microsoft straight

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:45.860
after that. So, a very simple path.

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.520
Host: Pretty straight.

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:52.310
You know, one of the things I’m hearing
from researchers all across the organization

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:57.270
is that a lot of what they are doing is very
interdisciplinary and they are working with

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:02.060
people that aren’t necessarily all from
the same field they came from and that there’s

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:05.160
a lot of wonderful cross-pollination.

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:07.050
Are you finding that in your work as well?

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:08.050
Antonio Criminisi: Absolutely.

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:12.870
You know, the cross-disciplinarity is, you
know, one of the biggest things, you know,

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:18.020
the best things you can do for innovation,
really, and it’s not just me who says that.

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:24.780
It is incredibly rewarding also to be able
to learn new things from people who don’t

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:30.020
necessarily speak exactly the same language
as you or don’t do exactly the same things as you do.

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:35.980
It’s, you know, it’s a great growing experience,
learning experience, and at the same time,

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:43.260
this sort of cross-disciplinary interaction
has got a lot of, you know, provides a lot

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:46.220
of impetus into innovation really.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:53.460
Host: As a researcher, part of your life is
just discovery and asking questions and then

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:56.160
digging in and finding what you find.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:00.920
And sometimes you get one topic as a life
work and other times it’s like, “Okay

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.380
here’s another thing that I’d like to
chase after.”

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:08.540
Do you have any other projects, or research
interests, on the go right now?

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:12.070
Antonio Criminisi: I have way
too many to share.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:19.260
But at the moment I would like to just be concrete
and deliver something of great value on this project.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:23.820
So that’s why for the last couple of years or so,
I’ve been focusing only, and entirely, on this project.

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:24.720
Host: Yeah.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:28.760
You’ve got a bunch in your brain that once
you deliver you can move onto.

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:29.760
Antonio Criminisi: That’s right.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:33.010
Host: It’s like the writer who has a hundred
stories they want to tell, but…

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:34.580
Antonio Criminisi: Yeah, one at a time.

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:35.580
Host: Yeah.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:40.650
It’s interesting to me that your framework
is super-practical and that isn’t always

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:42.710
the case, which is great.

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:46.400
You want researchers to be looking at things
from a variety of angles.

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:52.420
But I imagine that radiologists appreciate
the singular focus of what you are doing

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:53.800
to make their lives better.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:55.000
Antonio Criminisi: Yeah, that’s right.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.740
I think it’s very, very important.

00:23:56.740 --> 00:24:00.700
If you want to deliver something concrete,
and really, you’ve got to focus.

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:05.800
And that’s difficult to do believe it or
not because you have to learn to say no,

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.860
as well as yes.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:13.580
We get flooded with requests, all the time,
from many different doctors, many different

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:17.880
hospitals or organizations saying, “Hey,
I’ve read about InnerEye.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:19.620
You are doing great work.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:24.180
Can that technology be applied to
problem X and Y?”

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:29.930
And, more often than not, the answer is, “Yes
in theory, it could be applied in those other

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:32.520
domains, but I cannot do it!

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.540
I have to say no, because you know, the resources,
of course, are limited and time is limited.”

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:37.540
Host: Yeah.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:40.460
“Sorry, I’d like to say but I have to
say no.”

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:42.540
Oh, gosh.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:48.410
So, any thoughts as we close our Skype session
and you go off to happy hour and I get a second

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:50.660
cup of coffee here in Seattle?

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:53.780
Antonio Criminisi: No, I’m happy!

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:59.360
Host: Antonio, thank you so much for taking
time out of your day to join us on the podcast.

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:01.410
And it’s great talking to you.

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:04.310
Antonio Criminisi: Thank you very much.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:09.330
Host: To learn more about Dr. Antonio Criminisi,
and how machine learning technologies are

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:15.180
helping medical professionals provide better
healthcare, visit Microsoft.com/research.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:17.180
(music)

